earning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 27 Jan 2026]\nL9: # Title:Scale-Consistent State-Space Dynamics via Fractal of Stationary Transformations\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Geunhyeok Yu , cite6†Hyoseok Hwang L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Scale-Consistent State-Space Dynamics via Fractal of Stationary Transformations, by Geunhyeok Yu and Hyoseok Hwang\nL14: \nL15: cite7†View PDF cite8†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Recent deep learning models increasingly rely on depth without structural guarantees on the validity of intermediate representations, rendering early stopping and adaptive computation ill-posed. We address this limitation by formulating a structural requirement for state-space model's scale-consistent latent dynamics across iterative refinement, and derive Fractal of Stationary Transformations (FROST), which enforces a self-similar representation manifold through a fractal inductive bias.\nL17: Under this geometry, intermediate states correspond to different resolutions of a shared representation, and we provide a geometric analysis establishing contraction and stable convergence across iterations. As a consequence of this scale-consistent structure, halting naturally admits a ranking-based formulation driven by intrinsic feature quality rather than extrinsic objectives.\nL18: Controlled experiments on ImageNet-100 empirically verify the predicted scale-consistent behavior, showing that adaptive efficiency emerges from the aligned latent geometry.\nL19: Comments:  | 8 pages (excluding 2 pages of references), 3 tables, 2 figures. Appendix: 4 pages\nL20: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite9†arXiv:2601.19551 [cs.LG]\nL22:    | (or cite10†arXiv:2601.19551v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL23:    | cite11†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.19551†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Geunhyeok Yu [cite12†view email ]\nL27: [v1]