42†Which authors of this paper are endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite43†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL160: \nL161: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite44†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL162: cite45†About†info.arxiv.org · cite46†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite47†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite48†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite49†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite50†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite51†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite52†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L163: \nL164: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2601.19106v1] Detecting and Correcting Hallucinations in LLM-Generated Code via Deterministic AST Analysis (https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.19106v1)\nciteturn31155view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.19106v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Software Engineering\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 27 Jan 2026]\nL9: # Title:Detecting and Correcting Hallucinations in LLM-Generated Code via Deterministic AST Analysis\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Dipin Khati , cite6†Daniel Rodriguez-Cardenas , cite7†Paul Pantzer , cite8†Denys Poshyvanyk L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Detecting and Correcting Hallucinations in LLM-Generated Code via Deterministic AST Analysis, by Dipin Khati and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Large Language Models (LLMs) for code generation boost productivity but frequently introduce Knowledge Conflicting Hallucinations (KCHs), subtle, semantic errors, such as non-existent API parameters, that evade linters and cause runtime failures. Existing mitigations like constrained decoding or non-deterministic LLM-in-the-loop repair are often unreliable for these errors.\nL17: This paper investigates whether a deterministic, static-analysis framework can reliably detect \\textit{and} auto-correct KCHs. We propose a post-processing framework that parses generated code into an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) and validates it against a dynamically-generated Knowledge Base (KB) built via library introspection. This non-executing approach uses deterministic rules to find and fix both API and identifier-level conflicts.\nL18: On a manually-curated dataset of 200 Python snippets, our framework detected KCHs with 100\\% precision and 87.6\\% recall (0.934 F1-score), and successfully auto-corrected 77.0\\% of all identified hallucinations. Our findings demonstrate that this deterministic post-processing approach is a viable and reliable alternative to probabilistic repair, offering a clear path toward trustworthy code generation.\nL19: Comments:  | Accepted to FORGE 2026\nL20: Subjects:  | Software Engineering (cs.SE); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite11†arXiv:2601.19106 [cs.SE]\nL22:    | (or cite12†arXiv:2601.19106v1 [cs.SE] for this version)\nL23:    | cite13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.19106†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | cite14†https://doi.org/10.1145/3793655.3793725†doi.org DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Dipin Khati [cite15†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Tue, 27 Jan 2026 02:16:37 UTC (284 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Detecting and Correcting Hallucinations in LLM-Generated Code via Deterministic AST Analysis, by Dipin Khati and 3 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite9†View PDF L37:   * cite10†HT