 cite52†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite53†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite54†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L160: \nL161: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2601.19115v1] FBSDiff++: Improved Frequency Band Substitution of Diffusion Features for Efficient and Highly Controllable Text-Driven Image-to-Image Translation (https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.19115v1)\nciteturn31155view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.19115v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 27 Jan 2026 (this version), latest version 12 Jun 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:FBSDiff++: Improved Frequency Band Substitution of Diffusion Features for Efficient and Highly Controllable Text-Driven Image-to-Image Translation\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Xiang Gao , cite7†Yunpeng Jia L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled FBSDiff++: Improved Frequency Band Substitution of Diffusion Features for Efficient and Highly Controllable Text-Driven Image-to-Image Translation, by Xiang Gao and 1 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF L16: > Abstract:With large-scale text-to-image (T2I) diffusion models achieving significant advancements in open-domain image creation, increasing attention has been focused on their natural extension to the realm of text-driven image-to-image (I2I) translation, where a source image acts as visual guidance to the generated image in addition to the textual guidance provided by the text prompt.\nL17: We propose FBSDiff, a novel framework adapting off-the-shelf T2I diffusion model into the I2I paradigm from a fresh frequency-domain perspective.\nL18: Through dynamic frequency band substitution of diffusion features, FBSDiff realizes versatile and highly controllable text-driven I2I in a plug-and-play manner (without need for model training, fine-tuning, or online optimization), allowing appearance-guided, layout-guided, and contour-guided I2I translation by progressively substituting low-frequency band, mid-frequency band, and high-frequency band of latent diffusion features, respectively.\nL19: In addition, FBSDiff flexibly enables continuous control over I2I correlation intensity simply by tuning the bandwidth of the substituted frequency band.\nL20: To further promote image translation efficiency, flexibility, and functionality, we propose FBSDiff++ which improves upon FBSDiff mainly in three aspects: (1) accelerate inference speed by a large margin (8.9$\\times$ speedup in inference) with refined model architecture; (2) improve the Frequency Band Substitution module to allow for input source images of arbitrary resolution and aspect ratio; (3) extend model functionality to enable localized image manipulation and style-specific content creation with only subtle adjustments to the core method.\nL21: Extensive qualitative and quantitative experiments verify superiority of FBSDiff++ in I2I translation visual quality, efficiency, versatility, and controllability compared to related advanced approaches.\nL22: Subjects:  | Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)\nL23: Cite as:  | cite9†arXiv:2601.19115 [cs.CV]\nL24:    | (or cite10†arXiv:2601.19115v1 [cs.CV] for this version)\nL25:    | cite11†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.19115†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Xiang Gao [cite12†view email ]\nL29: [v1] Tue, 27 Jan 2026 02:39:20 UTC (24,333 KB)\nL30: cite5†[v2] Fri, 12 Jun 2026 12:33:30 UTC (25,478 KB)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: \nL34: ##